al integers 1..420 with edge x->y if x|y and y>x, and record remainder.\n# Find a path (not necessarily simple) that hits all 210 remainders, then it can repeat 0 forever.\n\n# We'll construct greedily: start a_1=1. To visit remainder r next, if gcd(r_prev,210)|r we can\n# go directly (multiply). Otherwise, first go to a multiple of current that has remainder r_prev's\n# multiple... Let's use: to switch to any new remainder, go current -> (current * 210) [remainder 0]\n# is dead. Instead go current -> current*something to reach remainder that divides target.\n\n# Cleaner: Let's use the \"multiply by 210\" to jump but that gives 0. \n# Alternative known construction: a_i = i (for i=1..209) does NOT satisfy divisibility.\n# Use a_i = L_i where L_i = lcm(1,2,...,i) * i? Let's test a_i = lcm(1..i).\nfrom math import lcm\ndef trial():\n    seq=[]\n    L=1\n    for i in range(1,210):\n        L=lcm(L,i)\n        seq.append(L)\n    # check divisibility and remainders\n    bad=0\n    rems=[]\n    for i in range(1,len(seq)):\n        if seq[i-1] % seq[i] !=0:\n            bad+=1\n    return bad\nprint(\"lcm chain divisibility failures (should be 0):\", trial())\n\n# lcm(1..i) is divisible by lcm(1..i-1) since it's a multiple. Yes! So a_i=lcm(1..i) gives a_i|a_{i+1}.\n# Now compute remainders lcm(1..i) mod 210 for i=1..209, plus handle 0.\nseen=set()\nprev=1\nL=1\nrems=[]\nfor i in range(1,210):\n    L=lcm(L,i)\n    r=L%210\n    rems.append(r); seen.add(r)\nprint(\"distinct remainders from lcm(1..i), i=1..209:\", len(seen))\nprint(\"missing:\", sorted(set(range(210))-seen))\nprint(\"0 in remainders?\", 0 in seen)\n# lcm(1..i) for i>=7 is divisible by 210 -> remainder 0! So 0 appears.\n# Do we get all 210? lcm grows; many remainders. Let's see."}) - MainThread
