2026-01-01 00:00:00,000[utu.hooks.base_hooks] - DEBUG - base_hooks.py:41 - [toolcall-call_6b213b01ed1349cb93a92097] execute_python_code({"code": "# Let me verify the formula with an EXACT brute force using a different method.\n# \n# min_turns(k): min turns to remove exactly k coins.\n# \n# Key theorem: min_turns(k) = smallest m such that sum of m largest piles >= k.\n# Proof sketch:\n#  - Lower bound: In m turns, we remove coins from at most m distinct piles \n#    (re-touching a pile wastes a turn). The max we can remove from any set of m piles\n#    is their total. To maximize, pick the m largest. So k <= sum of m largest.\n#  - Upper bound (achievability): Greedy (largest first) removes sum of m largest in m turns.\n#    If k <= sum of m largest, remove k coins: take the largest pile(s) fully until \n#    remaining <= current pile, take the rest from that pile. Done in m turns.\n#\n# Both bounds give min_turns(k) = smallest m with sum of m largest >= k. CONFIRMED.\n#\n# Let me verify with a DP that computes min_turns exactly for small n.\n# DP over subsets: for each subset S of piles, sum(S) is coins if we remove all of S.\n# But we can remove PARTIAL from one pile. So:\n# min_turns(k) = min over m of [there exist m piles and a partial removal achieving k]\n#             = min m such that k <= sum of m largest piles.\n#\n# Let me just do a clean brute force with memoized recursion that's efficient.\n\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nimport sys\nsys.setrecursionlimit(100000)\n\ndef brute_min_turns(k, piles):\n    \"\"\"DP: min turns to remove exactly k coins. Use the structure:\n    We choose a set of piles to remove from. From each chosen pile we remove some coins.\n    One pile can be the 'partial' one (removed less than full), others full.\n    Actually, min turns = min number of piles we touch, where we can remove any amount\n    from each (1 to full). This equals smallest m with sum of m largest >= k.\"\"\"\n    # Efficient: try all m, check if k <= sum of m