tation counts each friend-room assignment exactly once.\n# Let me verify: for a given friend-room assignment, the pattern is determined.\n# \n# Let me count differently. Consider assigning friends to rooms directly.\n#   Choose 2 friends for room 0: C(5,2) = 10\n#   Choose 2 friends for room 1: C(3,2) = 3\n#   Choose 1 friend for room 2: C(1,1) = 1\n#   Rooms 3,4 empty.\n#   But wait, this requires room 0,1 to have 2 and room 2 to have 1.\n#   Total for this specific arrangement = 10*3*1 = 30\n#   Number of ways to choose which rooms have the pattern {2,2,1}:\n#     Choose 2 rooms for the pairs: C(5,2) = 10\n#     Choose 1 room for the single: C(3,1) = 3\n#     = 30 arrangements\n#   Total = 30 * 30 = 900 ✓\n\n# Great, so the correct count is 900 for (2,2,1). My earlier formula overcounted because\n# I used 60 arrangements instead of 30.\n# \n# The correct way to count arrangements of occupancy multiset {2,2,1} into rooms:\n#   = number of ways to choose which rooms get count 2 (2 rooms): C(5,2) = 10\n#   * which room gets count 1: C(3,1) = 3\n#   = 30. \n#   OR equivalently 5!/2! = 60 is WRONG because... hmm.\n#   Actually 5!/2! counts ordered arrangements of the multiset, but since two counts are equal,\n#   we get 5!/2! = 60 arrangements. But then when we assign friends, we should NOT re-distinguish\n#   the two pair-rooms, which we don't because friends are assigned to specific rooms.\n#   \n#   The discrepancy is: 60 * 30 double counts. If I use 30 room-arrangements, I need friend-assignment\n#   = 5!/(2!2!1!) = 30, giving 900. \n#   If I use 60 room-arrangements, friend-assignment should be 5!/(2!1!) = 60 (treating pair rooms as distinct),\n#   giving 60*60 = 3600. Neither is right either way...\n#\n# Let me just trust the direct computation: 2220.\n\n# Let me verify the pattern counts with the cleaner combinatorial approach:\n# (2,2,1): choose 2 rooms for pairs (C(5,2)=10), choose 1 room for single (C(3,1)=3) => 30 room-arrangements\n#         friend assignments: choose 2 for first pair-room (C(5,2)=10), 2 for second pair-room (C(3,2)=3), 1 for single (1) => 30\n#         total = 30*30 = 900 ✓\n# (2,1,1,1): choose 1 room for pair (C(5,1)=5), remaining 4 rooms get singles\n#         friend assignments: choose 2 for pair-room (C(5,2)=10), rest 3 go to remaining rooms (3! = 6) => 60\n#         total = 5 * 60 = 300?? but direct says 1200!\n\n# Hmm, discrepancy again. Let me reconsider.\n# For (2,1,1,1): 4 rooms used, each room is distinct.\n# choose which room gets 2: C(5,1) = 5\n# friend assignment: choose 2 friends for that room: C(5,2) = 10, then assign remaining 3 friends to remaining 3 rooms: 3! = 6\n#   = 10 * 6 = 60\n# total = 5 * 60 = 300\n# But direct says 1200. Factor of 4 discrepancy!\n\n# The issue: for (2,1,1,1), the 4 rooms used are all distinct, and I only chose which room gets the pair (5 ways),\n# but I should also account for the arrangement. Wait, no. Let me reconsider.\n# 5 distinct rooms. Choose which one has 2 friends: 5 ways. The other 4 rooms each get exactly 1 friend.\n# For friends: choose 2 for the pair-room: C(5,2)=10. Assign remaining 3 friends to the 4 remaining rooms... \n# wait, there are 4 remaining rooms but only 3 friends! \n# \n# OH. I see my error. In pattern (2,1,1,1), only 4 rooms are used (one empty). \n# So: choose which room is EMPTY: 5 ways. Then choose which room gets 2: 4 ways. Remaining 3 rooms get 1 each.\n# OR: choose which room gets 2: 5 ways, then 4 rooms remain, choose which gets... no.\n#\n# Let me redo: pattern (2,1,1,1) means one room has 2, three rooms have 1, one room empty.\n#   Choose the empty room: 5 ways\n#   Choose the pair room from remaining 4: 4 ways\n#   = 20 arrangements\n#   Friend assignment: choose 2 for pair room: C(5,2)=10, assign 3 remaining friends to 3 remaining rooms: 3!=6\n#   = 60\n#   total = 20 * 60 = 1200 ✓\n\n# Great, 1200 matches.\n\n# (1,1,1,1,1): all rooms used, 1 friend each.\n#   = 5! = 120 ✓\n\n# Total = 900 + 1200 + 120 = 2220 ✓\n\nprin